                    "message": "\"Breaking change\"?  What repositories would need to be adjusted?\n\nI don't see anything wrong with making these more proper, we just need to do that appropriate refactoring at the same time."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Debug/Pid/ThermalPower.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "YouPengWu",
                        "email": "twpeng50606@gmail.com",
                        "username": "YouPengWu"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Debug/Pid/ThermalPower.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "I don't know why your reverted the enum change without getting to the conclusion on this discussion.  Since this is a \"Debug\" dbus interface anyhow, I don't even understand what \"breaking change\" would mean.  As I said earlier, we should have no problem transitioning interfaces to a more-correct definition.\n\nPlease either wait for @ed@tanous.net to give more clarity and/or put back the use of enums."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Debug/Pid/ThermalPower.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "YouPengWu",
                        "email": "twpeng50606@gmail.com",
                        "username": "YouPengWu"
                    },
                    "message": "At that time, my thinking was that the control system can be divided into open-loop control (stepwise controller) and closed-loop control (PID controller).\nFrom that perspective, putting open-loop\u2013related behavior into the Pid.ThermalPower interface felt conceptually a bit odd to me, which is why I hesitated and reverted the change."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Debug/Pid/ThermalPower.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Acknowledged.  I see now that what you've done is eliminate the \"Class\" property which was going to be an enum.  It looks like there aren't any string properties except for the \"Leader\" itself."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Debug/Pid/ThermalPower.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "YouPengWu",
                        "email": "twpeng50606@gmail.com",
                        "username": "YouPengWu"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes \u2014 before this patch, the real-time monitoring in phosphor-pid-control only supported PID (closed-loop) control, and ClassType was used to distinguish the sensor class (e.g. temperature).\n\nLater on, I refactored this by separating Class and Type into two independent concepts:\n\t\u2022\tClass represents the sensor category\n\t\u2022\tType represents the control method (stepwise or PID)\n\nIn the current design, the control method (Type) is now distinguished by using different interfaces, so ClassType is only needed to identify the sensor class.\nHowever, since stepwise currently only supports temperature sensors, the ClassType property is temporarily unused. This was discussed in the PID Discord channel as well."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Debug/Pid/ThermalPower.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 21,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Because these are two controllers that implement almost orthogonal interfaces, I suspect this would be a case where we should split and have a Debug.Stepwise.ThermalPower interface.\n\nAs written, this is still in the PID namespace."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Debug/Pid/ThermalPower.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 21,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "YouPengWu",
                        "email": "twpeng50606@gmail.com",
                        "username": "YouPengWu"
                    },
                    "message": "I have updated the code, commit message, and validation logs.\n\nI\u2019ve implemented xyz.openbmc_project.Debug.Stepwise.ThermalPower to ensure there is no impact on the existing xyz.openbmc_project.Debug.Pid.ThermalPower interface.\n\nPlease note that the Setpoint property is not applicable to Stepwise controllers, but the other properties align with the standard PID controller interface.\n\nPlease review. Thanks."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Debug/Pid/ThermalPower.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 21,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "YouPengWu",
                        "email": "twpeng50606@gmail.com",
                        "username": "YouPengWu"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                }
            ],
